diff -ruN a/src/HookChain.hpp b/src/HookChain.hpp
--- a/src/HookChain.hpp	2026-10-24 14:17:51.396539525 +0000
+++ b/src/HookChain.hpp	2026-10-24 14:32:54.865102861 +0000
@@ -11,17 +11,83 @@
 //   * when it is running, the hook exists, so a null slot is only that install window: wait it out.
 // chainNext() is only for code running inside the detour of that slot. Code that may run when the hook
 // failed must use chainPeek() and handle nullptr.
+//
+// PHASE 0 NOTE ON THE WAIT ITSELF
+// -------------------------------
+// The wait below is unbounded by necessity, and that is a deliberate conclusion, not an oversight:
+//
+//   1. Why the slot can be null: only the install window described above. The target is already patched
+//      to the chain when our detour first runs; rebuildChain() stores our slot microseconds later.
+//   2. Is there a valid fallback? No. The slot holds either the next mod's detour or the engine
+//      trampoline. Neither is knowable from anywhere else, and a detour cannot reconstruct it.
+//   3. So the only alternatives to waiting are (a) calling a null or stale pointer - immediate crash or
+//      silent corruption, or (b) returning without calling through - which skips the engine's own
+//      RenderLevel / BlockSource::getBlock and every other mod in the chain, changing game behaviour.
+//      Both are worse than waiting, so neither is done.
+//
+// What Phase 0 does change: the wait no longer burns a core, and it is now observable.
+//   * after kSpinBeforeYield attempts the waiter calls sched_yield(), so a long window cannot starve
+//     the very thread that is about to store the slot (the old loop was a pure busy spin);
+//   * every wait that actually had to spin is counted, and the longest one is recorded, so an abnormal
+//     install or chain rebuild shows up in the perf log instead of presenting only as a freeze.
+//
